  "worked_solution": "The number of bees $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $20%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=2\\,000$ is the initial number of bees.\n\n- $r=0.2$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in months, since the start of the study\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =2\\,000\\cdot (1+0.2)^{t} \\\\  & =2\\,000\\cdot (1.2)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it takes  for the hive to increase to $5\\,000$  bees, we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\,000 \\\\ 2\\,000\\cdot (1.2)^{t} & =5\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
